Overview
This lab focused on retrieving multiple pieces of information when the vulnerable application exposed only a single column in its response.
At first, this appeared to be a limitation because the target data existed in separate database columns. However, the solution demonstrated that multiple values can be combined into a single output through string concatenation, allowing them to fit within the structure expected by the original query.

Technical Concept
A UNION-based SQL injection attack requires the injected query to match the structure of the original query.
In this scenario:
* The application exposed only one visible column.
* Sensitive information existed across multiple database columns.
* The challenge was to transform multiple values into a single value that could be displayed by the application.
This was achieved by concatenating values together and separating them with a recognizable delimiter.

Key Learning
The most important lesson from this lab was understanding that successful exploitation often depends on working within the rules of the target system.
The database was not being tricked into ignoring its structure.

Instead:
* Column requirements remained enforced.
* Data type compatibility remained enforced.
* Query structure remained enforced.
The attack succeeded because the injected query respected those requirements while manipulating how data was presented.

Security Insight
This exercise highlighted that SQL injection vulnerabilities are not simply the result of malicious payloads.
They occur when applications allow untrusted input to influence database queries without proper controls.
Even when database rules are functioning correctly, insecure query construction can expose sensitive information through legitimate database behavior.

Reflection
This lab helped me move beyond thinking of SQL injection as a collection of payloads.
Instead, I began viewing it as an interaction between:
* Application logic
* Database structure
* Query constraints
* Output rendering
Understanding these relationships provided a clearer mental model of how UNION-based SQL injection works in practice.

